{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life involved complex political maneuvers and military conflicts that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. He was known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to navigate the treacherous waters of feudal politics while facing external threats from Muslim powers.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II was assassinated by the Order of Assassins. This tragic event forced the young count to assume leadership at a difficult time when the county faced instability. He spent his early years consolidating power and securing alliances with neighboring lords to protect his territory from invasion. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him additional lands and influence within the kingdom. This union strengthened his position and allowed him to become one of the most powerful barons in the region. He often acted as a regent or advisor to kings who were either minors or unable to rule effectively due to illness or captivity.\n\n*Political Struggles and Regency*\nThe count frequently found himself at odds with other factions within the kingdom especially regarding succession disputes. He opposed Guy of Lusignan becoming king because he believed Guy lacked the necessary experience and judgment to lead during such perilous times. Raymond served as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not always perform his royal duties. During this period he tried to maintain peace with Saladin while preparing defenses against potential attacks. His cautious approach sometimes drew criticism from more aggressive members of the nobility who wanted immediate military action against Muslim forces. Despite these tensions he remained a central figure in court politics and military planning.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaymond eventually retired from active politics after failing to prevent the rise of Guy of Lusignan to the throne. He spent his final years in Tripoli where he focused on administering his own county rather than intervening in kingdom-wide affairs. His death occurred shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin which resulted in the loss of Jerusalem to Saladin. Historians often view him as a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader power better than his contemporaries. His efforts to preserve the kingdom through diplomacy rather than reckless warfare demonstrated his strategic mind. The collapse of the kingdom soon after his passing highlighted the value of his steady leadership during previous decades of conflict and uncertainty.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.00010097537}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0017873516}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.01633042}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.016323693}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -9.274913e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0994463e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.014635025}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.005936866}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"tok